1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
A density is a curve whose area is probability. The normal is the one with a square in the exponent.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Mu slides the curve sideways; sigma squeezes it. The area stays one at every pair.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The peak is one over sigma root two pi: 0.3989 at sigma 1, 0.1330 at sigma 3, 0.0266 at sigma 15.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
GATE CSE 2026 Set 2 printed a density and asked which family it is: the constant and the 18 make it N(0,9).

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
z = (x minus mu) over sigma: how many standard deviations x sits from the mean.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
P(X at most x) = Phi of z. One table then serves every normal distribution.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
Phi(0) = 0.5, Phi(1) = 0.8413, Phi(2) = 0.9772, Phi(3) = 0.9987, and Phi(-1) = 0.1587.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Phi has no closed form. The page integrates the density twice, by two different rules, and they agree.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Symmetry: Phi(-z) = 1 - Phi(z), and the page checks it at six values of z.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
An interval is Phi(b) minus Phi(a); a two-sided band is 2 Phi(z) minus 1.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Top 5 per cent means Phi(z) = 0.95, so z = 1.6449. Top 2.5 per cent is z = 1.9600.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Divide by sigma, never by sigma squared; and take the square root when a variance is given.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Within one sigma: 0.6827. Within two: 0.9545. Within three: 0.9973.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
The rule is an integration, so it holds for every mu and every sigma without adjustment.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Beyond three sigma lies 0.0027; beyond four sigma, 6.33 times ten to the minus five.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
GATE 2016 GA: two equal groups, sigma 25 against 5. The narrower range belongs to the smaller sigma.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
X is N(1,4). The threshold -1 sits one sigma below the mean, so P(X at most -1) = Phi(-1) = 0.1587.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Y is N(-1, sigma squared). The threshold 2 sits 3 over sigma above its mean, so P(Y at least 2) = 1 - Phi(3/sigma).

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
A left tail equalling a right tail makes the two z values negatives, so 3/sigma = 1 and sigma = 3.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The two wrong routes: using the variance 4 as a sigma, and matching P(X at most -1) with P(X at least 1).

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
aX + b is normal with mean a mu + b and variance a squared sigma squared.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Add 5 to every mark: the mean moves from mu to mu + 5 and sigma does not move at all.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
Independent normals add: N(1,4) + N(2,9) is N(3,13), and the convolution agrees to four places.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Standardising is the same rule with a = 1/sigma and b = -mu/sigma, which is why z is always N(0,1).

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
For a normal curve the mean, the median and the mode are all mu, because the curve is symmetric.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
A median is any m with P(Y at most m) at least 0.5 and P(Y at least m) at least 0.5.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Y = max(X,0) puts mass 0.5 at zero: P(Y at most 0) = 0.5 and P(Y at least 0) = 1, so the median is 0.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
GATE CSE 2017 Set 1 Q19 asks exactly this and wants 0. The reason is the atom, not the symmetry of X.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
The central limit theorem: a standardised sum of independent copies tends to N(0,1), whatever the shape of one copy.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Measured on sums of exponentials: the largest gap from Phi is 0.1587 at n = 1, 0.0945 at n = 2, 0.0596 at n = 5.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
At n = 10 it is 0.0421, at 30 it is 0.0243, at 100 it is 0.0133. Slow, monotone, and never exactly zero.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
So normality is a conclusion about sums and averages, and an assumption when applied to one measurement.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
de Moivre-Laplace: a binomial with mean np and standard deviation root npq is nearly normal.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
P(X at most 45) at n = 100, p = 0.5: exact 0.184101, corrected 0.184060, uncorrected 0.158655.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The correction is the half-step: the bar at k covers k minus 0.5 to k plus 0.5, so the edge is k + 0.5.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
It buys four decimal places here and about two at n = 25, p = 0.2 — a skew needs the larger n.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Same mean, same variance, different probabilities: P(|X| at most 1) is 0.5774, 1 and 0.6827.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
A mixture of two normals is symmetric with mean and median 0, and has two modes at plus and minus 2.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
A mean of n observations has spread sigma over root n: sigma 10 with n = 100 gives a standard error of 1.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
The procedure: standardise, read Phi with the sign the question set, then say which assumption you used.

